-- ADVANCED / MEDIUM — Stimulus: Ethical Consumerism
(gen_random_uuid(),'MULTIPLE_CHOICE',9,'READING','Comprehension – Literal',3,
'Ethical consumerism is the practice of making purchasing decisions based on moral principles, particularly regarding social justice, environmental sustainability and animal welfare. Consumers who subscribe to this philosophy may choose to buy Fairtrade certified products, ensuring producers in developing countries receive fair payment. They may opt for locally sourced goods to reduce the carbon footprint associated with transportation, or avoid products tested on animals. The rise of ethical consumerism has prompted many companies to adopt more responsible practices, or at least to present themselves as doing so — a phenomenon critics call "greenwashing". The effectiveness of ethical consumerism as a force for change is debated: while individual choices can signal demand for ethical products, structural changes in regulation and corporate accountability are often argued to be more impactful at scale.','TEXT',
'ADVANCED','MEDIUM',
'What is "greenwashing" according to the passage?','{"A":"A new type of environmentally-friendly manufacturing","B":"The process of certifying products as ethically produced","C":"Companies presenting themselves as responsible without genuinely being so","D":"A regulation that forces companies to reduce their environmental footprint"}','{"value":"C"}','The passage defines greenwashing as companies presenting themselves as responsible, which critics point out may not reflect genuine practice.','PUBLISHED',NOW(),NULL),
