-- PREMIUM / HARD — Stimulus: Geopolitics and Critical Minerals
(gen_random_uuid(),'MULTIPLE_CHOICE',9,'READING','Comprehension – Literal',5,
'The distribution of natural resources has long been a source of geopolitical tension. Nations rich in oil, rare earth minerals or fresh water hold strategic advantages in the global order, while resource-poor countries must navigate complex trade relationships. The transition to renewable energy introduces new resource pressures: technologies such as electric vehicles and solar panels depend on critical minerals including lithium, cobalt and nickel — resources concentrated in relatively few countries. This creates new forms of resource dependency even as the world attempts to reduce its reliance on fossil fuels. Analysts warn that competition for critical minerals could reshape alliances and provoke conflicts, much as competition for oil shaped the geopolitics of the twentieth century. Equitable access to resources, and the economic benefits they generate, remains one of the central challenges of our time.','TEXT',
'PREMIUM','HARD',
'Which minerals are specifically mentioned as critical for renewable energy technologies?','{"A":"Iron, aluminium and copper","B":"Lithium, cobalt and nickel","C":"Gold, silver and platinum","D":"Uranium, thorium and plutonium"}','{"value":"B"}','The passage specifically lists lithium, cobalt and nickel as critical minerals for electric vehicles and solar panels.','PUBLISHED',NOW(),NULL),
